      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rixford was named after the Rixford family, possibly honoring Samuel Rixford who helped plat the settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rixford_pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village sprang up during the late 19‑century oil boom, turning timberlands into a petroleum hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rixford_pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1880, Rixford had 1,127 residents and a railroad and gas pipeline linking it to Duke Center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rixford_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A devastating fire on May 9, 1880 destroyed 75 buildings and 70,000 barrels of oil, but rebuilding began immediately.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rixford_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rixford sits at 1,598 feet on the Allegheny Plateau, surrounded by northern hardwood forests that cover 88 % of McKean County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rixford_pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community experiences a humid continental climate with cold, snowy winters and mild summers, averaging 39 inches of snow each year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
